{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=}}
'''Kaikōura''' (or '''Kaikoura''' before 2008) is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning a single MP to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Kaikōura is [[Stuart Smith (politician)|Stuart Smith]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], who won the {{NZ election link|2014}}.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Kaikōura.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The Kaikōura electorate covers the north-eastern South Island, from [[Cook Strait]] in the north to the [[Ashley River / Rakahuri]] in the south. At over {{convert|21000|km2|abbr=on}}, it is New Zealand's fourth-largest general electorate by area. The electorate is named after the town of [[Kaikōura]] near the centre of the electorate. Its biggest town is [[Blenheim, New Zealand|Blenheim]]; other towns include [[Amberley, New Zealand|Amberley]], [[Ashley, New Zealand|Ashley]], [[Cheviot, New Zealand|Cheviot]], [[Culverden]], [[Goose Bay, New Zealand|Goose Bay]], [[Hanmer Springs]], [[Havelock, New Zealand|Havelock]], [[Hawarden, New Zealand|Hawarden]], [[Hurunui, New Zealand|Hurunui]], [[Picton, New Zealand|Picton]], [[Rai Valley]], [[Renwick, New Zealand|Renwick]], [[Seddon, New Zealand|Seddon]], [[Waipara]], [[Waiau, Canterbury|Waiau]], [[Wairau Valley]] and [[Ward, New Zealand|Ward]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/kaik%C5%8Dura-electorate-profile/ |title=Kaikōura - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 22 September 2025}}</ref> The electorate boundaries were not changed in the 2007, 2013/14, 2020 or 2025 boundary reviews.<ref name="Electorate profiles – historical, 2011">{{cite web |title=Kaikōura: Electoral Profile |url=http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/electorates/2011/data/DBHOH_Lib_EP_Kaikoura_Data_3/kaik%C5%8Dura-electoral-profile |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=26 September 2014 |date=30 September 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121063429/http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/electorates/2011/data/DBHOH_Lib_EP_Kaikoura_Data_3/kaik%C5%8Dura-electoral-profile |archive-date=21 January 2016 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=22 September 2025 |page=22 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
Kaikōura is one of the original 60 electorates drawn ahead of the change to [[Mixed-member proportional representation|Mixed Member Proportional]] (MMP) voting in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]]. It was made up by merging all of the old [[Marlborough (New Zealand electorate)|Marlborough]] seat with a large portion of [[Rangiora (New Zealand electorate)|Rangiora]]. Like the two electorates it replaced, Kaikōura is a safe seat for the National Party, returning a National MP at every election since it was created.

The first representative in 1996 was [[Doug Kidd]], who was previously the MP for [[Marlborough (New Zealand electorate)|Marlborough]]. He retired at the end of the parliamentary term and was succeeded by [[Lynda Scott]] in the {{NZ election link|1999}}. Scott served for two parliamentary terms before retiring from politics and returning to the medical profession in 2005.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Dr Lynda Scott not seeking re-election |url= http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0407/S00353.htm |access-date=18 October 2010 |agency=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |publisher=[[New Zealand National Party]] |date=21 July 2004}}</ref>

The {{NZ election link|2005}} was won by [[Colin King]], who served for three parliamentary terms.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/former/50PlibMpsFormerColinKing1/king-colin |title=Colin King |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=25 September 2014 |date=22 September 2014}}</ref> In December 2013, King was deselected as National's candidate for Kaikoura, losing a selection challenge by [[Stuart Smith (politician)|Stuart Smith]],<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/9528694/Kaikoura-MP-deselected-by-National |title=Kaikoura MP deselected by National |work=[[Stuff.co.nz]] |date=17 December 2013 |access-date=25 September 2014}}</ref> who won the general election in September {{NZ election link year|2014}} with a preliminary majority of 11,510 votes.<ref>{{cite web|title=Election Results – Electorate Status |url=http://2005.electionresults.govt.nz/electoratestatus.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=20 September 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090425152328/http://2005.electionresults.govt.nz/electoratestatus.html |archive-date=25 April 2009 }}</ref> Based on preliminary election results, [[Steffan Browning]] of the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] who became a list MP in {{NZ election link year|2011}}, was the [[Party lists in the 2014 New Zealand general election#Green Party|highest ranked]] Green candidate who was not returned to parliament.<ref>{{cite web |title=Provisional List of Successful Candidates – 2014 General Election – Preliminary Results |url=http://2005.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/successfulcand.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=24 September 2014 |date=21 September 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075348/http://2005.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/successfulcand.html |archive-date=6 October 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bell |first1=Cathie |title=National's Stuart Smith wins Kaikoura |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/marlborough-express/10525470/Nationals-Stuart-Smith-wins-Kaikoura |access-date=24 September 2014 |work=[[The Marlborough Express]] |date=20 September 2014}}</ref> When final results were released, the Green Party had gained an additional seat, and Browning was confirmed as a list MP.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rutherford |first1=Hamish |title=National loses majority, Greens pick up one |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/election-results-2014/10580208/National-loses-majority-Greens-pick-up-one |access-date=4 October 2014 |work=[[Stuff.co.nz]] |date=4 October 2014}}</ref>

